# 🏗️ How Do You Design Infrastructure That Empowers Developers Without Giving Them Footguns?

---

# 🎯 Core Philosophy

My goal is:

> Enable developer self-service while enforcing safe production guardrails automatically.

Platform engineering should:

* reduce cognitive load
* standardize operations
* prevent dangerous patterns
* accelerate delivery safely

---

# 🏗️ Recommended Architecture

```text id="gnk3oi"
Developers
     ↓
Self-Service Platform
     ↓
Golden Templates / GitOps
     ↓
Policy Enforcement
     ↓
Secure Kubernetes / Cloud Infrastructure
```

---

# ✅ Key Design Principles

---

# 1. Golden Paths

Provide pre-approved templates for:

* services
* CI/CD pipelines
* Terraform modules
* Helm charts
* observability
* security policies

Developers should consume:

```text id="r0a0tr"
approved building blocks
```

instead of reinventing infrastructure.

---

# 2. Policy-As-Code

Use:

* OPA Gatekeeper
* Kyverno
* Sentinel

to automatically enforce:

* resource limits
* approved registries
* security contexts
* network policies
* tagging standards

---

# 3. Least Privilege Access

Developers should:

* deploy applications
* view logs
* scale workloads

but should NOT:

* modify cluster networking
* alter IAM
* bypass security controls

---

# 4. GitOps-Based Delivery

Use:

* ArgoCD
* FluxCD

Benefits:

* auditability
* rollback
* drift detection
* controlled deployments

---

# 5. Multi-Tenant Isolation

Use:

* namespaces
* network policies
* dedicated node pools
* quotas
* Pod Security Standards

to avoid noisy neighbors and privilege escalation.

---

# 6. Guardrails Instead of Manual Reviews

Automate:

* security scanning
* Terraform validation
* admission control
* image verification
* secret detection

The best platform:

```text id="fjlwm7"
prevents bad deployments by design
```

---

# 🎯 Real Production Example

Instead of allowing raw Kubernetes YAML:

* developers use Helm templates
* policies validate manifests
* CI blocks insecure configs
* ArgoCD deploys automatically

This balances:

```text id="grrjlwm"
speed + safety
```

---

# 🐧 Linux-Level Checklist Before Approving Any Custom AMI to Production

---

# 🎯 Goal

Ensure:

* security
* reliability
* observability
* immutable reproducibility
* operational consistency

---

# ✅ AMI Validation Checklist

---

# 1. OS Hardening

Verify:

* CIS benchmarks
* unnecessary packages removed
* root login disabled
* password auth disabled
* sudo restrictions

---

# 2. Kernel & Package Validation

Check:

```bash id="3njgx8"
uname -r
rpm -qa
apt list --installed
```

Ensure:

* latest security patches
* kernel compatibility
* no experimental modules

---

# 3. systemd & Boot Reliability

Validate:

```bash id="ynnt4d"
systemctl --failed
journalctl -b
```

Check:

* failed services
* persistent journald
* cloud-init completion
* startup dependencies

---

# 4. Container Runtime Validation

Validate:

* containerd/docker version
* CRI compatibility
* kubelet integration
* cgroup driver consistency

---

# 5. Security Controls

Verify:

* SELinux/AppArmor
* auditd
* Falco/EDR agent
* file integrity monitoring
* SSH hardening

---

# 6. Observability

Confirm:

* logs persist across reboot
* node-exporter works
* metrics accessible
* tracing agents functional

---

# 7. Kubernetes Compatibility

Check:

* kubelet startup
* CNI compatibility
* CSI drivers
* IP forwarding
* sysctl values

---

# 8. Performance & Stability

Stress test:

* CPU
* disk IO
* memory
* network throughput

Watch for:

* kernel panic
* OOM
* cgroup instability

---

# 9. Immutable Infrastructure Validation

AMI should be:

```text id="7z6f4t"
fully reproducible via automation
```

Never manually patched.

---

# 🎯 Production Rule

If AMI creation cannot be:

* reproduced
* versioned
* validated automatically

it should NOT go to production.

