# 📊 Moving from Centralized Logging to Service-Mesh-Based Observability

---

# 🎯 First Clarification

A service mesh does NOT replace centralized logging completely.

It primarily enhances:

* telemetry
* tracing
* traffic visibility
* service identity

---

# 🏗️ Architecture Comparison

## Traditional Model

```text id="ncyhr7"
Application
    ↓
Fluent Bit / Log Agent
    ↓
Central Logging Platform
```

---

## Service Mesh Model

```text id="icfl9l"
Application
    ↓
Envoy Sidecar
    ↓
Distributed Telemetry
    ↓
Tracing / Metrics / Traffic Policies
```

---

# ✅ Benefits of Service Mesh Observability

---

# 1. Distributed Tracing

Provides:

* request path visibility
* latency analysis
* dependency mapping

Tools:

* Jaeger
* Tempo
* Zipkin

---

# 2. Automatic Telemetry

Without modifying applications:

* latency metrics
* retries
* error rates
* mTLS visibility

---

# 3. Security Visibility

Track:

* east-west traffic
* service identity
* unauthorized calls

---

# ⚠️ Tradeoffs

| Benefit            | Tradeoff            |
| ------------------ | ------------------- |
| Deep observability | Higher complexity   |
| mTLS support       | CPU/memory overhead |
| Traffic control    | Sidecar management  |
| Better tracing     | Debugging harder    |

---

# ⚠️ Operational Concerns

Potential problems:

* Envoy sidecar explosion
* increased memory usage
* startup latency
* certificate rotation issues
* noisy telemetry

---

# 🎯 My Production Approach

I prefer:

```text id="8zjlwm"
hybrid observability
```

Use:

* centralized logging for forensic retention
* service mesh for telemetry/tracing
* Prometheus for metrics
* SIEM for security analytics

